For this version, all existing node pointers are saved in a slice. That makes the new order very clear: the node at `length - k` becomes the head, and the nodes before it move after the old tail.

For `1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4` and `k = 1`, the order of pointers becomes `4, 1, 2, 3`. The last node must point to `nil`, otherwise an old link could leave a cycle.

```go
/*
Definition for singly-linked list.
type ListNode struct {
    Val  int
    Next *ListNode
}
*/

func rotateRight(head *ListNode, k int) *ListNode {
    if head == nil || head.Next == nil || k == 0 {
        return head
    }

    nodes := make([]*ListNode, 0)
    current := head
    for current != nil {
        nodes = append(nodes, current)
        current = current.Next
    }

    length := len(nodes)
    k %= length
    if k == 0 {
        return head
    }

    newHeadIndex := length - k
    newHead := nodes[newHeadIndex]

    for i := newHeadIndex; i < length-1; i++ {
        nodes[i].Next = nodes[i+1]
    }
    nodes[length-1].Next = nodes[0]
    nodes[newHeadIndex-1].Next = nil

    return newHead
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the slice of node pointers.

An empty list and rotations that are multiples of the length are handled before any links are changed.

The fast pointer is moved `k` nodes ahead of the slow pointer. Then both pointers move together until fast reaches the last node. The node after slow is the new head.

This avoids making a circular list. The only detail to handle first is `k %= length`, since a full loop around the list has no effect.

```csharp
/*Definition for singly-linked list.
public class ListNode {
    public int val;
    public ListNode next;
    public ListNode(int val=0, ListNode next=null) {
        this.val = val;
        this.next = next;
    }
}
*/

public class Solution {
    public ListNode RotateRight(ListNode head, int k) {
        if (head == null || head.next == null || k == 0) {
            return head;
        }

        int length = 0;
        ListNode current = head;
        while (current != null) {
            length++;
            current = current.next;
        }

        k %= length;
        if (k == 0) {
            return head;
        }

        ListNode fast = head;
        for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) {
            fast = fast.next;
        }

        ListNode slow = head;
        while (fast.next != null) {
            fast = fast.next;
            slow = slow.next;
        }

        ListNode newHead = slow.next;
        slow.next = null;
        fast.next = head;
        return newHead;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

The links are rearranged in place. No extra array or set is used.

A right rotation by `k` places the last `k` nodes in front. First find the length, then make the list circular and cut it after the node that should become the new tail.

The modulo step matters because rotating a list of length `n` by `n` positions gives the original list. It also keeps very large `k` values manageable.

```java
/*Definition of singly linked list:
class ListNode {
    int val;
    ListNode next;

    ListNode() {
        val = 0;
        next = null;
    }

    ListNode(int data1) {
        val = data1;
        next = null;
    }

    ListNode(int data1, ListNode next1) {
        val = data1;
        next = next1;
    }
}
 */

class Solution {
    public ListNode rotateRight(ListNode head, int k) {
        if (head == null || head.next == null || k == 0) {
            return head;
        }

        int length = 1;
        ListNode tail = head;
        while (tail.next != null) {
            tail = tail.next;
            length++;
        }

        k %= length;
        if (k == 0) {
            return head;
        }

        tail.next = head;
        int move = length - k;
        ListNode newTail = head;

        for (int i = 1; i < move; i++) {
            newTail = newTail.next;
        }

        ListNode newHead = newTail.next;
        newTail.next = null;
        return newHead;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

Empty input, a one-node list, and `k` equal to a multiple of the list length all return without changing the list.

The list can be treated like a circle for a moment. After counting the nodes, connect the tail to the head, move to the new tail, and cut the circle there.

For `1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4 -> 5` and `k = 2`, the new tail is `3`, so the result starts at `4`.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    ListNode* rotateRight(ListNode* head, int k) {
        if (head == nullptr || head->next == nullptr || k == 0) {
            return head;
        }

        int length = 1;
        ListNode* tail = head;
        while (tail->next != nullptr) {
            tail = tail->next;
            length++;
        }

        k %= length;
        if (k == 0) {
            return head;
        }

        tail->next = head;

        int stepsToNewTail = length - k;
        ListNode* newTail = head;
        for (int i = 1; i < stepsToNewTail; i++) {
            newTail = newTail->next;
        }

        ListNode* newHead = newTail->next;
        newTail->next = nullptr;
        return newHead;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`. The input list is changed by reconnecting its links.

